lly among\nwomen of color. AftieanvAmericon\nand Hispanic women account for 75\npercent of female AIDS cases, al-\nthough they represent tess than\nonefourth of all U.S. women. the\n(CDC reported.\nSplvia Hepler is a member of the\nHarrisburg Foundation's AIDS\nFund advisory board and executive\nirector for the AIDS Planning Coa-\nition of South Central Pennsylva-\nnia, She bas worked with A{DS and\nHIV patients and recognizes the\ncomplexities of their nutritional\nneeds.\n\"Lthinke that persons with HIV/\nAIDS have a tremendous need for\nhhuteitional counseling and. educa-\ntion,\u201d she said. \u201cThey need to un\nderstand. what type of food is best\n\u2018going to fuel their body.\u201d\n\u2018Because HIV infection requires a\ntremendous emount of energy. ped:\nple with the virus who do not eat\norzectly may experience dramatic\nweightloss.\n\u2018While eating correctly is impor-\ntant, it isn'ta panacea,\n\"ust because an infected person\n\u2018eats well and drinks wel. that does\nmean that a person is going to be\ncured from HIV infection.\u201d she\nsald, \u201cGood nutrition does not\nequal a cure for this. But good nt\ntition will in many cases increase\nlife and reduce your suffer\n\nBELLSeLL0z",
